{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790531516,"nonce":"kFzd3oG1ruQzfqW5Hqp/d9Zns98=","title":"aot: a second emit_aot in one process reuses the first program's bodies","message":"The optimized-AIR cache (air_pipeline OPTIMIZED), the LLVM ceiling cache (llvm::air::llvm_ceiling) and the loaded PGO targets are process-wide and keyed by findex or caller. emit_aot never cleared them, so a host that builds twice without restarting (caribou serve rebuilding on a source change) lowered the second program from the first one's AIR, linked against the second one's data.\n\nSeen in caribou: a string-constant edit adds one global, so every later global slot moves by one. The rebuilt swarm.wasm had plain.hl's code (Type's class read at slot 59) and edited.hl's data (slot 59 is the new string constant). Type.init saw a non-null $allTypes, skipped hballoc, and hl_hb_resize asserted at module init. A fresh caribou build of the same .hl was correct.\n\nReproducer: crates/ash/tests/aot_twice.rs builds B, A, then B again in one process. Before the fix the third build panicked compiling the entry point (\"Could not get field of non-object type\"); after it the two B objects are byte-identical.\n\nHot reload already cleared the AIR cache but not the ceilings; it now clears both.","files":null}]}